I've actually been working on the design for a flexible FPGA board for
developing SSD replacements for all sorts of QBUS storage (Unibus
ought to work too, though you'd need to build a different card).  The
chief obstacles to doing so seem to actually be the QBUS drivers
themselves; if you want to be totally compliant to DEC's specs, they
need to be open-collector drivers that can sink 70mA and limit their
slew rate to 10ns or more.  This, for those uninitiated into the
wonderful world of signal propagation, is to limit ringing and thus
false transitions on the bus.

Once that's been solved, the QBUS interface is actually not
particularly complex; a medium-sized FPGA (Cyclone III, maybe an
EP3C40) should be WAY more than enough to handle whatever logic you
need for the QBUS interface and the card logic itself (including a
large variety of open-source cores for internal microprocessors).
Slap an SD/MMC interface on the backend, and you've got an interface
that'll work for everything from hard disks to tape drives; an RS232
interface or even an LCD control panel would let you, for example,
chose from among multiple tape images on a large SD card and
manipulate things like the emulated drive's online settings.

In any case, I haven't had the time to actually put the hardware
together (I've found line drivers that meet most of the specs that are
still in production), but I hope I can soon; it should be a flexible
board to work with and fairly easy to adapt to anything.  What's the
general level of interest on this, so I can know how many boards I'd
need to build?


