Elderico wrote:

> is there anything else I have to do to achieve that? 

In the output of your SYSGEN SHOW/CLUSTER you posted a couple days ago,
MSCP seemed to be turned off.

Normally, cluster config would turn it on. Not sure why it was off.

If a satellite node gets the NISCS_LOAD.EXE with its parameters appended
to it, it should start to issue at least some message. (I think).

YOu could try:

> Parameters in use: Active
> Parameter Name            Current    Default     Min.     Max.     Unit  Dynamic
> --------------            -------    -------    -------  -------   ----  -------
> VAXCLUSTER                      2          1         0         2 Coded-valu
> NISCS_CONV_BOOT                 1          0         0         1 Boolean
> NISCS_LOAD_PEA0                 1          0         0         1 Boolean
> MSCP_LOAD                       1          0         0     16384 Coded-valu
> MSCP_SERVE_ALL                  1          4         0        15 Bit-Encode

Remember that to modify the parameters of your satellite node from the
boot node, you issue:

MC SYSGEN
SYSGEN> USE disk:[SYS10.SYSEXE]vaxvmssys.par
(or whatever root number if it is not 10)
SYSGEN> SET NISCS_CONV_BOOT 1
SYSGEN> SET MSCP_LOAD 1
SYSGEN> SET MSSP_SERVE_ALL 1
SYSGEN> WRITE disk:[SYS10.SYSEXE]vaxvmssys.par
SYSGEN> EXIT

NISCS_CONV_BOOT enables you to enter the SYSBOOT> utility (SYSGEN during
very early stages of boot) utility.

MSCP_LOAD  tells the booting node to start the MSCP software. This si
teh sofwtare that lets one node access the disks that are on another
node. This is REALLY required for a satellite node.

TO get to conversational boot:

B/1 device  (I can't remember off hand what the device name is for
ethernet booting on a MVII).

After the MOP transaction, you should quickly get a SYSBOOT> prompt. If
it doesn't get that far, then it is likely a hardware config problem.




