JF, I tried to do three things in that email:
1) summarize what we've done and what we know for sure
2) reduce the hardware configuration of the microVAX to the bare
minimum (and I couldn't remember whether the Q bus was a terminated
bus or not ;-)
3) use another boot mode because LANCP on a virtual VAX on a linux
host may have issues. I don't know that, but I when in doubt use
familiar tools and for me that means DECnet.

That said, the OP moved those memory boards around and if it is faulty
memory then it is very likely the 1 MB on-board memory and only two 8
MB boards will get that error go away. It seems the most obvious
conclusion but what bothers me is the lack of error messages. The
system boots, no errors and the OP gets a clean srm prompt, did I get
that right?
If so there's something else wrong. Hence the suggestion to move to
DECnet and good old NCP where you can turn on monitoring events.
Hans


