> of something like `cfdisk` command on linux systems)

If you want to know the volume label then you can mount a disk (a tape
too BTW) as a foreign device.
The command is : $ MOUNT/FOREIGN $1$DUA0:
The disk spins up and when it encounters no internal problems will
return its label. The resident filesystem is not interpreted.
When you're sure the disk holds a VMS filesystem but you don't know
its label then this will work:
$ MOUNT/OVERRIDE=IDENT $1$DUA1:
In case the system ran ultrix or BSD then VMS can't mount them. The
way to make the disk available for VMS is;
$ INIT/SYSTEM $1$DUA1: DATA               ! DATA is the label and a
clean filesystem is written to the disk
$ MOUNT/SYSTEM $1$DUA1: DATA         ! the /SYSTEM qualifier keeps the
disk mounted and online when you log off

When both disks are useful then I'd recommend putting the VMS*.* kit
files on DUA1:, Mount/foreign Dua0 and back/ima vms*.B/save dua0:
When that is ready you can boot from dua0 and proceed with the
installation of VMS.
Hans

PS
All command examples assume that you are logged in as SYSTEM or an
equivalently privileged account.


