For what you're doing SYSTEM is the perfect account!

The error message doesn't do anything for me unfortunately. What does
make me wonder is that a MOUNT/FOREIGN as well as an INIT give the
same error message, which surprises me. The thing is, I buy second
hand SCSI disks for my VAXstations. These disks are seldom, actually
never used as VMS disks.The filesystems on them may have been for Sun,
linux and who knows what other operating systems we have. A foreign
mount always works and this usually results in an error message like
"No Files-11 home block found" and an attempt to return what VMS
thinks was the disks' label.
An INIT always works. If it doesn't you get "device off line" and
between 10 and 10**3 errors logged. That way you know the disk is
dead.
Your situation is different and perhaps the RD54 has been formatted
not with 512 B blocks but with another scheme. It's not partitioning
because VMS ignores that information and overwrites it with its own
data.
A microVAX 2000 can do a low level format of an RD54 (and 53) disk.
That might help.
But you mentioned moving jumpers on the RD54's, right? Before you
travel that road please check whether you moved a jumper that affected
the way the disk is sectored.
Hans


