Do you speak VHDL? I'm a mostly-software type recently forcibly
introduced to the world of FPGAs. These things are amazing, and
amazingly cheap, especially if you have access to someone who already
knows lots more VHDL than I currently do :(

You can probably find someone's FPGA development board with most of
the electronics you need for roughly $100 new. Then it's a mere matter
of programming. It's a well known fact that all software projects work
first time and come in under budget, especially when using a
technology new to the developers, so if you turn this into mostly-
software rather than mostly-hardware, what could possibly go wrong?

There seem to be some pages out there about FPGA implementations of
Apple II including disk emulators, which might give you some ideas.

The Datex product Rob already mentioned is from a range which uses a
microprocessor for the clever stuff and an FPGA for the donkeywork
[1], which may perhaps be because they're not a recent design. Modern
FPGAs can easily implement a microcontroller inside, even a full
microprocessor in the right circumstances. FPGA designs should be easy
to share too, if you want this to be something that other people can
use.

Then again if you'd rather breadboard than keyboard, FPGAs may not be
the approach for you.

Have fun anyway!

[1] http://translate.google.co.uk/translate?hl=3Den&sl=3Dfr&u=3Dhttp://www.=
datexdsm.com/emulator/docs/EMULATEUR.pdf


