Elderico wrote:

> woa! seems a bit complicated.. I'm a newbie with VMS, and I'll have to
> study this a bit!


The normal way to install VMS on a node is to have a local CD device
attached. YOu boot a minimal VMS from the CD, and are ab;e to use the
BACKUP utility to initialise the system disk and populate it with a
minimal VMS on it.

Then, when you reboot the VAX, it knows that it is in the middle of a
VMS installation and completes the installation by reading from the CD
the remainingt components that need to be installed.

When a machine doesn't hacve a CD or other media from which it can
install VMS, the easiest way is to cluster this "naked" node as a
satellite of a VMS node that has access to the media.


So the MOP request that comes from the naked node gets a response of a
file which consists of a compnination of the "very minimal VMS", along
with its system parameters. With this, it join the VMS cluster, and
start to access the main node's drives as if they were local. (at which
point, it has no problem completing the boot and/or accessing CDs etc.

The problem is that while a LInux node may be able to respond to a MOP
request with a single file, for a VMS environment, the MOP request
contains a couple of files appended to each other. So it isn't so easy
to emulate.

It may sound complicated, but once you understand the principles, it isn't.

And configuring a cluster isn't.

You will want to configure your simh node as a cluster first. You give
your cluster a password. Then you use the same cluster_config procedure
to add a satellite node (your naked vax). You give it a name, node ID,
and then it will prompt you for its ethernet address. It will configure
thing on the boot node side, and then it will ask you to boot the naked
vax, at which point the simhg node will be able to respond to the boot
request make let the vax boot into the cluster.


