Considering that this is a MicroVAX II, the Q-bus interrupt grant chain 
is always suspect.  Are you sure that your cards are all configured in a 
way that meets the Q-bus rules?

The number one rule is that there must not be any gaps in the interrupt 
chain.  The interrupt chain is serpentine (wanders from top to bottom, 
side to side) in the backplane and you must have either a card or a 
grant repeater in every position from the CPU to the last card in the 
chassis.

NOTE: The path of the serpentine chain varies depending on whether you 
have an 8-slot backplane or a 12-slot.

The other thing that will get you in trouble are the CSR/Vector 
settings.  Nearly all Q-bus cards have a CSR address that must be set 
with switches or jumpers.  Many also require that the Vector be set. 
The documentation for each card needs to be checked.

Since your Ethernet controller is responding, its CSR is set correctly. 
  I think the Vector on these cards is set automagically, so you don't 
need to worry about that.  However, if some other card in the machine is 
set to a conflicting Vector, you could have problems.

When I have problems like this, I usually reduce the cards in the 
MicroVAX to CPU, 1 memory card, 1 disk controller, 1 ethernet 
controller.  Get this working and then add one card at a time until you 
get your desired configuration working.

Fore more information, check:

http://hoffmanlabs.org/openvms/hwvax/hwqbus.shtml

Good luck!

