On the SIMH vax (the boot node)

$ mc lancp

LANCP> show node

Node Listing, volatile database:

(Catchall entry):

BIKE1 (08-00-2B-11-1F-81):
  MOP DLL:  Load file:   NISCS_LOAD.EXE
            Load root:   $4$DIA5:<SYS10.>
            Boot type:   VAX satellite
LANCP>



The above says that the boot node is willing to serve MOP requests from
a machine with the ethernet address specified, and called "BIKE1".

The MOP response will start with the NISCS_LOAD.EXE file, but also
include other stuff after it, namely the directory root for node BIKE1.

Whden NISCS_LOAD runs on the satellite node, it established a SCS
connection to the boot node and uses MSCP to access the actual boot
code.  This early part isn't "clustering" yet.

Your failure happens at a very early stage, before licences are checked
or the cluster authorisation is checked.

In the above example, the satellite node would epect to find its
operating system under the disk $4$DIA5:[SYS10]

So for instance, when looking for the VMS SYSGEN parameters, it would
ask for $4$DIA5:[SYS10.SYSEXE]VAXVMSSYS.PAR

For normal files, it has enough smarts to ask for 2 locations:
$4$DIA5:[SYS10.directory]file.ext  as well as:
$4$DIA5:[SYS10.syscommon.directory]file.ext

This is why there is an alias entry called "syscommon" in each root
which points to the common system files located in $4$DIA5:[VMS$COMMON]


What you want to do is verify that the LANCP database points to a
directory structure which exists and is populated with some basic files
in the  "sysexe" directory (aka: $4$DIA5:[SYS10.SYSEXE] )

LANCP uses < and > but interactively, you use [ and ].

Next, you will want do do this:

MC SYSGEN
SYSGEN> USE $4$DIA5:[SYS10.SYSEXE]VAXVMSSYS.PAR
SYSGEN> SHOW/CLUSTER and perhaps post the results here.


