Booting off a Simh host is probably the best way. Best way, because
the simulated VAX behaves as a documented platform so you can use all
the VMS documentation, available on-line at the HP site.
Your main problem may be finding a functional RD54 on the system.
They're not well known for having long lives (at least not in my
experience).
On a mVAX 2000 the led on an RD54 displays disk activity, so I'd
remove the disk which has this led always on from the system. You
wrote that both disks give the same error code, 4C. Assuming that
either just one disk is bad, or if they're both bad then they suffer
from different problems (because they show different behaviour), so I
hope that 4C stands for "cannot find a valid boot block".
Good luck,
Hans


